FILE TRANSFER AND PROTOCOLS

FILE TRANSFER PROTOCOL(FTP):
    |--> CONNECTION OVER TCP
          CONTROL PORT (21) DATA PORT (20)
          MODES: ACTIVE/PASSIVE
          COMMANDS:
            |--> GET - Retrieves a file from the remote FTP server and stores it locally
                  PASS/PASSIVE - Switches the FTP server into passive mode
                  PWD - Prints the current working directory 
                  PUT - Stores a file on the remote FTP server
                  EXIT - Terminate FTP sessions and exit

FTP MODES: ACTIVE
    |--> BUYING A PIZZA, AND THE FTP SERVER BRINGING THE PIZZA TO YOUR SYSTEM

    PASSIVE:
    |--> BUYING A PIZZA, YOU GO TO GET IT AND THE FTP SEVER GIVES YOU A LOCATION TO PICK IT UP AT
          (MODE USED TO INTERACT WITH THE SERVER)


FILE TRANSFER PROTOCOL SECURE(FTPS):
    |--> FTP INTIGRATED WITH TLS/SSL
          MODES:
            |--> Explicit: client connects to the server’s control port (usually port 21) initially, and then negotiates the use of TLS/SSL encryption before proceeding with file transfers
                 Implicit: assumes encryption at all times and uses TCP port 990 for control and TCP port 989 for data with encryption happening on both channels.


SECURE COPY(SCP):
    |--> DATA TRANSFER OVER PORT 22
          SYMMETRIC AND ASYMMETRIC ENCRYPTION
          NON-INTERACTIVE
          ALTERNATE SSH PORT (-P) TELLS THE SCP WHAT PORT TO GO THROUGH 
          (EX. scp -p 6969 /full/path /full/path)
          
NETCAT MODES:
    |--> LISTENER: nc -lvp <RHP>
          CLIENT: nc <client ip> <RHP>

NETCAT FILE TRANSFER:
    |--> LISTENER: nc -lvp <RHP> > newfile.txt        (receive file)

    |--> CLIENT: nc 172.16.82.106 <RHP> < file.txt    (sends file)

